maria_docs: FYI opened a docs PR to add a couple of practical examples for function/tool calling + structured output. seeks: short migration note, SDK snippets (py/js), a parsing example for streaming, and a guardrails section. PR: https://github.com/redwood-inference/docs/pull/482 :eyes:

alex_r: thanks! will review. do we want both JSON Schema and a minimal 'hand-rolled' validator example? some customers asked for tiny JS parse helpers.

maria_docs: yes both. plan: JSON Schema as the canonical guardrail + small JS and py parsing helpers for streaming/partial output. keep the schema simple and focused on required fields.

kevin_eng: note: runtime emits function-call tokens as normal text until we hit the call boundary. add tip about confirming final chunk contains the closing JSON blob. e.g., expect final 'content' chunk with full JSON.

sam_sde: putting a quick migration blurb for SDK changes: v0.x -> v1.x call signature changed from functions: [..] to tool_calls: {..} — I'll draft. also will add snippet for using incremental parser with our python SDK.

maria_docs: sketching the canonical JSON Schema for the example. paste below, feel free to edit.

maria_docs: ```json
{
  "$schema": "http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#",
  "type": "object",
  "required": ["action", "params"],
  "properties": {
    "action": { "type": "string", "enum": ["search", "summarize", "create_ticket"] },
    "params": {
      "type": "object",
      "properties": {
        "query": { "type": "string" },
        "priority": { "type": "string", "enum": ["low","medium","high"] },
        "items": { "type": "array", "items": { "type": "string" } }
      },
      "additionalProperties": false
    }
  },
  "additionalProperties": false
}
```

alex_r: that looks good. suggest adding a short example of tolerant parsing for streaming (i.e., stitch partial frames until valid JSON). small JS helper would be useful.

kevin_eng: also add a small note about schema size: keep it compact to avoid token bloat. for big param blobs recommend supplying an object reference (id) instead of inline data.

sam_sde: adding the py streaming parser snippet here:```py
# tiny incremental parser for newline-delimited chunks that may have partial json
import json
buffer = ''
def feed(chunk):
    global buffer
    buffer += chunk
    try:
        obj = json.loads(buffer)
        buffer = ''
        return obj
    except json.JSONDecodeError:
        # not a complete json blob yet
        return None
```

maria_docs: and JS counterpart:```js
// simple accumulator for partial chunks
let buffer = ''
function feed(chunk) {
  buffer += chunk
  try {
    const obj = JSON.parse(buffer)
    buffer = ''
    return obj
  } catch (e) {
    return null // wait for more
  }
}
```

alex_r: for the SDK snippets, can we show the new call style? small py example:```py
from redwood import Client
client = Client()
resp = client.generate(
  model="gpt-like-instruct",
  input="Find top 3 bugs in this log and classify",
  tool_calls={
    "name": "triage_tool",
    "schema": {"$ref": "#/definitions/triage"}
  }
)
# resp.tool_output -> validated object matching schema
```

sam_sde: i'll add a short migration note: if you previously used functions param, replace with tool_calls and pass schema as 'schema' or 'schema_ref'. include a quick regex find/replace suggestion.

maria_docs: tiny guardrails section we should include: - prefer schemas with additionalProperties=false - set enums for known actions - avoid large example payloads inline - validate on client side and server side - include fallback user-facing messages if parsing fails :warning:

kevin_eng: nit: document behavior when validation fails mid-stream. recommend material: treat as parsing error -> fallback to default 'manual_hand_off' action and emit a log. keep short code example for fallback.

